  <Article>
    <ArticleHeader
      label="White Paper"
      title="The Social Paper"
      authors={['Alexandru Ifrim', 'Gabriel Pene']}
      date="2026-06-22"
    />

    <ArticleAside label="Abstract" italic>
      <p>
        <em>The Social Paper</em> examines the architectural and economic forces that
        transformed the World Wide Web from a decentralized network of human connection
        into an engine of surveillance capitalism.
      </p>
      <p>
        By tracing the historical absence of native privacy primitives, this
        document dissects how commercial social media platforms have exploited
        the Web's open DNA to prioritize algorithmic addiction, ad-driven
        capital gains, and corporate dominance over user agency.
      </p>
      <p>
        Furthermore, it addresses the systemic vulnerabilities that allow
        AI-driven bot networks to weaponize polarization, alongside the urgent
        need to design "safe by default" environments for the next generation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ultimately, this paper serves as a foundational blueprint for reclaiming
        the digital town square. It proposes a radical architectural shift
        toward "local-only" privacy processing, cryptographic human-identity
        verification, and the establishment of a transparent, open-source,
        non-profit organization.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is a fundamentally rebuilt social web: one engineered for human
        well-being, where users are protected, companies are mere guests, and
        capital gains are permanently removed from the equation.
      </p>
    </ArticleAside>

    <TableOfContents items={parts} />

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-i" part="Part I">
        The Architecture of Openness and the Lack of Privacy Primitives
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-necessary-architecture-of-openness"
        >The Necessary Architecture of Openness</H3
      >
      <p>
        When Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web in 1989, his primary
        objective was to solve a specific problem at CERN: the fragmentation of
        information across incompatible computer systems. The Web was designed
        as a radically open, decentralized system for researchers to share
        documents. Its foundational building blocks—HTTP, HTML, and URLs—were
        engineered for frictionless connectivity and data retrieval, built atop
        an Internet whose own interconnection depended on Border Gateway
        Protocol (BGP) routing between Autonomous Systems (ASNs)—the
        independently operated networks of Internet Service Providers (ISPs) of
        different tiers.
      </p>

      <p>
        Noticeably absent from these foundational protocols were native privacy
        primitives. There was no built-in mechanism for identity verification,
        no native encryption, and no concept of personal data sovereignty.
        Crucially, the Internet was not built with privacy in mind—<em
          >nor should it have been</em
        >. Over 35 years ago, this radical openness was a mandatory feature. The
        cryptographic tools and computational power required for native privacy
        would have crushed the fragile early network. It had to be frictionless
        to allow for rapid, decentralized innovation.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, as the Internet transitioned from an academic network to a
        commercial engine, the absence of privacy layers paved the way for an
        ecosystem dominated by surveillance capitalism. That does not mean every
        service on the Web requires the same privacy primitives—requirements are
        business-specific. But wherever user behavior and interests can be
        extracted, privacy primitives should be required. Those extractions are
        routinely used to serve targeted ads and curated news—and in doing so,
        platforms can push users toward more polarizing content, even when no
        one set out to divide them.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-scarcity-of-privacy-primitives"
        >The Scarcity of Privacy Primitives</H3
      >
      <p>
        A "primitive" in computer science is a basic building block upon which
        more complex systems are built. While the Web developed robust
        primitives for communication and data transfer, true privacy primitives
        remained largely nonexistent, retrofitted, or inadequate for consumer
        protection:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Cookies (1994):</U> Originally introduced by Netscape to solve a technical
          issue (allowing a shopping cart to remember items), cookies were quickly
          weaponized by ad networks into the primary vector for third-party tracking—and,
          as cookies were later restricted by legislation (e.g. ePrivacy, GDPR) and
          browser policy, similar persistence shifted to localStorage, sessionStorage,
          and IndexedDB.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>IPsec (1995):</U> Introduced to secure Internet Protocol communications
          by encrypting data packets at the network layer. While it became the robust
          backbone for Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) and enterprise-level network
          security, it did not solve consumer privacy on the Web. The social media
          platforms on the receiving end of the traffic still decrypt and harvest
          the raw data at the destination.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>SSL/TLS (HTTPS):</U> Introduced in the mid-1990s, this provided
          <em>security</em> (encrypting data in transit) but not <em>privacy</em
          > (the platforms still see and harvest the plaintext data upon arrival).
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>WireGuard (2015):</U> Designed as a modern, high-performance alternative
          to older VPN protocols like IPsec, WireGuard simplified secure tunneling
          through state-of-the-art cryptography and a remarkably small, auditable
          codebase. Yet, much like IPsec, it is a network-layer tunnel. It successfully
          secures the <em>transit</em> of data from your device to a server, but it
          does not prevent the ultimate destination (the social platform) from harvesting
          the decrypted payload once it arrives.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>End-to-End Encryption (E2EE):</U> While true privacy-first apps use E2EE
          as an unremovable foundational primitive, commercial platforms have historically
          treated it as a disposable feature.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Mobile:</U> The same scarcity played out on mobile: advertising identifiers
          (IDFA, GAID), on-device app storage, and third-party SDKs became primary
          vectors for behavioral extraction—met only later with restrictions like
          App Tracking Transparency.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Because privacy was not a structural primitive of the Web, it had to be
        bolted onto the sides of commercial platforms. As the digital economy
        matured, platform architects realized that engineering true privacy into
        the system would directly cannibalize their primary source of capital:
        targeted ads.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
        >The New DNA: Local-Only Primitives</H3
      >
      <p>
        To fix this, the architecture must shift from centralized corporate
        servers to local processing—meaning the user's own hardware handles the
        computational load. True privacy primitives for the modern social web
        must be built entirely around a "local-only" framework that securely
        spans the user's personal hardware ecosystem. This is a foundational
        architecture—not a finished privacy guarantee. Governance, query
        safeguards, and visible user control must still be woven in:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only feed building:</U> At the platform level, public posts & content
          are processed with machine learning so they can be matched to what users
          are looking for, then stored in searchable databases on the platform. However,
          instead of a centralized server profiling the user to decide what they should
          see, this matching process is inverted. The user's device locally computes
          an "intent"—a secure, on-device representation of their current interests
          or search parameters—and sends it to the server to run the matching pipeline
          for that feed request. The server does not retain intent to build a profile,
          and it does not learn which content they engaged with from on-device interaction
          history.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only settings:</U> By enforcing local-only settings, user preferences
          (blocks, themes, filters) act as an on-device firewall, preventing platforms
          from mining "privacy toggles" as just another psychological data point.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Local-only tracking of user platform interaction:</U> The device locally
          observes user behavior to personalize the experience. If the platform needs
          to update its broader models, it must rely on volunteers helping it do so
          or by collecting anonymized data strictly from users who explicitly allow
          it.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Encrypted Cross-Device Synchronization:</U> To ensure a seamless user
          experience, these local states are shared exclusively between the user's
          own active sessions across their phone, tablet, laptop, and PC. This creates
          a secure, personal data mesh. Synchronization happens via zero-trust, end-to-end
          encrypted relays or direct peer-to-peer connections. The platform's servers
          act only as blind couriers; they never hold the master copy, the decryption
          keys, or the plaintext data. Signal and WhatsApp already demonstrate this
          for messaging: linked devices stay synchronized end-to-end encrypted across
          phone, tablet, and desktop.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Encrypted backups:</U> To protect against device loss without reverting
          to corporate cloud storage, local state is backed up end-to-end encrypted.
          Backup relays or user-controlled storage hold only ciphertext—the platform
          never holds the decryption keys or plaintext data. Signal has already proven
          this model for messaging backups; the same approach can extend to the broader
          social web.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-1" number={1} title="Improving the Web">
        <p>
          Improving the Web requires recognizing that while the foundational
          building blocks of the past cannot be changed, the DNA of the Internet
          is not static. To reclaim user agency and move beyond the era of
          surveillance capitalism, privacy can no longer be treated as a premium
          UI feature, an obscure setting, or a corporate promise. It must be
          woven directly into the Web's DNA.
        </p>

        <p>
          By demanding and implementing local-only feed building, local
          settings, local-only tracking of user platform interaction, encrypted
          cross-device synchronization, and encrypted backups, the tech industry
          can fundamentally break the centralized data-harvesting model while
          preserving connectivity.
        </p>

        <p>
          Signal and WhatsApp have already proven encrypted cross-device
          synchronization for messaging; Signal has done the same for encrypted
          backups. These are not untested ideas—the social web need only adopt
          what messaging has already demonstrated.
        </p>

        <p>
          Local-only processing is the necessary starting point, not the entire
          answer. Until privacy becomes a local, structural reality of the
          Internet's underlying code, it will remain a fragile illusion.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-ii" part="Part II">
        Hooked on the Feed and the Commodification of Connection
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-ii-social-media-hooked-on-the-feed"
        >Social Media: Hooked on the Feed</H3
      >
      <p>
        Social media platforms generate revenue by showing you ads. It is their
        core engine. The absolute vast majority of their multi-trillion-dollar
        valuations comes from harvesting identity and selling attention. Paid
        services and features account for a very small percentage of their
        bottom line.
      </p>

      <p>
        In their early days, platforms like Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram
        focused purely on user acquisition and connection. But once these
        companies went public, the stock market demanded perpetual growth in
        Average Revenue Per User (ARPU). To drive these capital gains, social
        media platforms made a critical architectural shift: they abandoned
        chronological timelines in favor of algorithmic feeds.
      </p>

      <p>
        The engineering mandate became simple—keep people hooked on the feed for
        as long as possible. The longer a user continuously scrolls, the more
        ads they can be shown. Independent analysis confirms that major
        platforms' algorithms reward engagement—and that outrage spreads before
        fact-checking can occur. In this environment, privacy and user agency
        are actively antithetical to the business model. Platforms designed user
        interfaces to encourage oversharing and perfectly tailor the addiction
        loop.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-algorithmic-interventions">Algorithmic Interventions</H3>
      <p>
        The most direct way to fix filter bubbles is to change the "goal" of the
        algorithms delivering the content.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Adjusting the Objective Function:</U> Instead of solely rewarding an
          algorithm for maximizing engagement (clicks, likes, watch time), developers
          can introduce penalties for over-serving the same type of content. The algorithm
          must be trained to value a balanced algorithmic diet.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>The Exploration vs. Exploitation Tradeoff:</U> Recommendation systems
          heavily favor "exploitation" (showing you what they know you like). To break
          echo chambers, systems need to dial up "exploration" (taking a risk by showing
          you something entirely new). This introduces planned serendipity into a
          user's feed.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Bridging-Based Ranking:</U> Instead of promoting content that gets a
          high reaction from a specific, polarized cluster of users, algorithms can
          be tuned to prioritize content that receives positive engagement across
          diverse, opposing user clusters. This elevates content that finds common
          ground.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-2"
        number={2}
        title="Adding the Social back in Social Media"
      >
        <p>
          For too long, the "social" in social media has been entirely
          overshadowed by the "media" of advertising. Social networks should not
          be vectors for capital gains, engineered solely to keep investors
          happy and stock prices climbing. Their fundamental purpose should be
          to serve as a vector for genuine human connection. The Internet was
          designed to be a global melting pot, a shared digital space where our
          differences are showcased to be cherished and celebrated. Instead, the
          current ad-driven models weaponize these differences, algorithmically
          optimizing for outrage to divide and polarize us simply because anger
          increases screen time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Commercial platforms stripped away user agency by abandoning
          chronological timelines and making outrage-optimized algorithmic feeds
          the default—and often the only—option. A healthier social network must
          restore that choice. Users should be able to select the feed that
          suits them: a reverse-chronological timeline by default, or a
          local-only algorithmic feed when they explicitly opt in. The algorithm
          must never be imposed; it must be a deliberate choice, with intent
          computed on the user's own devices and without surrendering engagement
          data to build a corporate behavioral profile.
        </p>

        <p>
          Platform-funded studies claiming that feed changes have little effect
          on polarization should be read with extreme skepticism. When the
          subject of the research funds and controls the experiment, buries
          opt-in privacy features, and alters algorithms mid-trial without
          disclosure, the conclusions serve the platform—not the public.
        </p>

        <p>
          To reclaim this original promise, the underlying ownership structures
          of these platforms must change. The digital town square should not be
          owned by a for-profit corporation beholden to the Stock Market.
          Instead, social media platforms should be stewarded by non-profit
          organizations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Furthermore, a social network's code should be open, not closed behind
          proprietary corporate walls. Open-source code allows a global
          community of developers, ethicists, and users to help the platform
          grow, audit its algorithms, and ultimately keep it honest. Only by
          combining non-profit stewardship, open-source transparency, and
          local-only privacy primitives can we finally add the "social" back
          into social media.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iii" part="Part III">
        The Next Generation: Easing Children into the Digital World
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-iii-the-legislative-wave">The Legislative Wave</H3>
      <p>
        The addictive, ad-driven architecture of modern social media has proven
        especially catastrophic for the developing minds of children.
        Recognizing this, a massive legislative wave has recently swept the
        globe. Over the last two years, governments have shifted from issuing
        warnings to enacting hard legal barriers. Australia has been at the
        forefront: since 10 December 2025, children under 16 have been barred
        from holding accounts on major platforms such as Instagram, TikTok, and
        YouTube, with fines of up to A$49.5 million levied on companies that
        fail to comply—while imposing no penalties on young people themselves.
        Nations ranging from the UK and France to the UAE are currently
        implementing their own age restrictions and mandatory parental consent
        laws.
      </p>

      <p>
        These laws come from a place of necessary urgency. However, as the
        United Nations and child psychology experts have pointed out, simply
        banning children from platforms is treating the symptom rather than
        curing the disease. UNICEF Australia agrees: the real fix is improving
        social media safety and listening to young people—not merely delaying
        their access. Enforcing these bans also pushes platforms toward
        behavioural inferencing and biometric age checks—the same profiling
        machinery this paper argues against.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-safe-by-design">Safe by Design</H3>
      <p>
        Completely isolating minors until their 16th birthday creates a
        dangerous cliff-edge scenario: a child goes from having zero digital
        exposure to being suddenly thrust into a highly manipulative,
        algorithmically hostile environment with no preparation. Because
        children are growing up in a digital-first world, they need to be
        carefully <em>eased</em> into social media, not just locked out of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Easing children into the digital town square means the spaces they
        inhabit must be "safe by design" from the ground up. This requires an
        entirely different set of rules for underage accounts. Children do not
        need algorithmic feeds optimized for endless scrolling; they need
        chronological feeds that allow them to step away naturally. They need
        the absolute guarantee, backed by local-only privacy primitives, that
        their behavioral data is not being harvested to build psychological
        profiles for targeted advertising.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-guardrails-for-minor-accounts"
        >Guardrails for Minor Accounts</H3
      >
      <p>
        Safe architecture alone is not enough. Where age restrictions,
        parental-consent requirements, or other minor-protection laws apply—as
        they already do in Australia, the UK, France, the UAE, and beyond—the
        platform will respect them. Compliance is not optional decoration; it is
        part of easing children in responsibly, rather than treating regulation
        and product design as opposing forces.
      </p>

      <p>
        Underage accounts will operate under deliberate limitations on the
        social graph. New connections—follows, direct messages, and other
        one-to-one or group links—require explicit approval from a verified
        parent or guardian before they take effect. A minor cannot freely expand
        their reach; every new link in the network passes through a parent.
      </p>

      <p>
        Public accounts introduce a further constraint. Only accounts that pass
        a platform whitelist—vetted against child-safety criteria—are eligible
        for minor interaction at all. That whitelist is a gate on eligibility,
        not the final word: even when a public account is cleared, a parent must
        still approve each specific connection before a minor can follow or
        engage with it. Two layers, not redundant checks.
      </p>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-3"
        number={3}
        title="Protecting the Vulnerable"
      >
        <p>
          Protecting the next generation requires more than a single fix. We
          must enforce digital age verification so we actually know when a minor
          is using a platform. We must stop treating children as a lucrative
          data demographic. Where legislation applies, we must respect it. And
          we must constrain the social graph itself—through a vetted whitelist
          of eligible public accounts and parent approval of every new
          connection—so that easing in never means exposure without supervision.
        </p>

        <p>
          We need to provide them with digital literacy education built natively
          into the platforms themselves, teaching them how to navigate online
          spaces, spot misinformation, and curate their own digital well-being.
          By redesigning the architecture to be safe by default, we can ease
          kids into the social fabric of the Internet, ensuring they learn how
          to connect and thrive before they are exposed to the deeper
          complexities of the open Web.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iv" part="Part IV">
        The Automation of Outrage and Reclaiming the Human Network
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-iv-the-ease-of-creating-and-managing-bots"
        >The Ease of Creating and Managing Bots</H3
      >
      <p>
        The radical openness that allowed the early Web to flourish has become
        the exact vulnerability currently destroying its social fabric. Because
        there is no structural identity verification on the modern web, creating
        and managing bots on social media has become trivially easy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Anyone with basic coding knowledge and a few dollars can spin up
        thousands of synthetic accounts using automated scripts and open APIs.
        Today, bots are not just spamming links; they are weaponized to game
        algorithmic feeds, acting as super-spreaders for propaganda,
        misinformation, disinformation, malinformation, and fake news. They
        exist to artificially amplify outrage and polarize the public.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-the-industry-of-social-media-farms"
        >The Industry of Social Media Farms</H3
      >
      <p>
        Bots are only half the problem. Across the globe, an entire industry of
        social media farms operates in plain sight: warehouses of phones,
        freelancers paid per engagement, and operators juggling dozens—or
        thousands—of accounts at once. These networks do not exist to converse.
        They exist to manufacture momentum. A single farm can swarm a post with
        likes and reposts until algorithms mistake manufactured hype for organic
        virality, pushing divisive content into millions of genuine feeds. The
        same infrastructure is used to polarize: coordinated accounts amplify
        outrage, drown out nuance, and create the illusion that extreme
        positions dominate the public square.
      </p>

      <p>
        Unlike fully automated bots, farm accounts are often operated by real
        people on real devices, which makes them harder for trust-and-safety
        teams to classify and remove. They exploit the same open architecture
        and engagement-driven ranking that bots do—only at industrial scale.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-ai-powered-bots-and-farms">AI-Powered Bots and Farms</H3>
      <p>
        Generative AI has supercharged both threats at once. Scripted bots no
        longer need hand-written scripts, and farm operators no longer need
        rooms of people typing identical replies. The same models that draft
        marketing copy can now run entire account networks—holding contextual
        conversations, mimicking human empathy, and producing flawless, highly
        persuasive text at an infinite scale. A single operator can manage what
        used to require a warehouse.
      </p>

      <p>
        Beyond text, generative AI has ushered in a new era of synthetic
        media—hyper-realistic deepfakes, fabricated photos, and completely fake
        videos that are now nearly indistinguishable from reality, bypassing
        human skepticism and rewriting it in real time. Because these bots act
        and sound like real users, and can seamlessly generate fake visual
        evidence of events that never happened, they are becoming nearly
        impossible to spot through traditional means and incredibly difficult
        for trust and safety teams to remove. The cost of generating a million
        synthetic opinions, paired with fabricated visual media, is effectively
        zero, allowing bad actors to entirely drown out genuine human discourse.
        Defending democracies against this threat requires structural
        verification, deliberation, and accountability—not content moderation
        alone.
      </p>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-4" number={4} title="People First, Bots Never">
        <p>
          To successfully defend the digital town square, the hierarchy of a
          healthy social network must be absolute: <strong
            >people first, bots never.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <p>
          That requires every account to be linked to a verified digital
          identity—cryptographically tying the account to a real person, so
          there is always a human behind it. When digital presence is
          accountable, consequence-free anonymity and inauthentic amplification
          can no longer dominate the network. This is not a perfect solution,
          but it is a meaningful improvement over the system we have today.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-v" part="Part V"> The Corporate Guest in a Human Space </H2>

      <H3 id="part-v-rebalancing-the-current-power-dynamic"
        >Rebalancing the Current Power Dynamic</H3
      >
      <p>
        In the current iteration of the Web, users are treated as the product,
        and corporations are treated as the primary customers. Social media
        architectures are optimized entirely to appease these commercial
        entities, granting them unfettered access to human attention and data.
        To grab and hold that attention, engagement-driven ranking tends to
        amplify polarizing and inflammatory content—material that provokes
        strong reactions and keeps users on the app longer so that more ads can
        be served. The platforms decide the defaults: algorithmic feeds over
        chronological timelines, buried privacy settings over visible control,
        corporate data retention over user sovereignty. They own the social
        graph, the ranking systems, and the unilateral right to change the
        rules—often announced in a policy update long after the product has
        already shifted beneath users' feet.
      </p>

      <p>
        This imbalance is not a failure of goodwill; it is a structural
        consequence of for-profit stewardship. Public social media companies owe
        a fiduciary duty to shareholders, not to the people using the platform.
        Privacy primitives, chronological feeds, and reduced engagement are not
        neutral product choices—they are direct threats to Average Revenue Per
        User. A for-profit board cannot voluntarily surrender the architectural
        levers that drive capital gains and expect to remain in office. As long
        as the venue is built to maximize shareholder value, users will always
        sit at the bottom of the hierarchy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rebalancing this dynamic requires reversing who the space is for. In a
        human social network, power must flow from verified people outward.
        Corporations may participate in the conversation, but they do not
        architect the venue, own behavioral data, or decide by default what
        humans see in their feeds. That reversal is architectural and
        economic—not a settings panel, a transparency report, or a promise to
        "do better." The platform must be governed for community welfare first;
        only then can companies be relegated to guests rather than overlords.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-pure-capitalism-and-social-capitalism"
        >Pure Capitalism and Social Capitalism</H3
      >
      <p>
        These are not abstract ideologies—they are two operating models for how
        a social platform can be run. <em>Pure capitalism on the social web</em> is
        the dominant model today: revenue from advertising and attention harvesting,
        growth and ARPU as the measures of success, and user data and engagement treated
        as inputs to capital gains. Algorithmic feeds are tuned to maximize time-on-app.
        Encryption, privacy controls, and feed choice exist only so long as they do
        not threaten revenue—when they do, they are buried, made optional, or removed
        entirely. Surveillance capitalism, as described throughout this paper, is
        the mechanism; pure capitalism names the governance outcome: shareholder value
        placed above community welfare.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Social capitalism on the social web</em> inverts that priority. The platform's
        primary obligation is to its community—not to profit margins, quarterly earnings,
        or investor returns. Capital is still required to operate the platform. Revenue
        from optional premium features and services, donations, sponsorships, and
        grants must fund maintenance, research and development, salaries, and bonuses—but
        that capital does not own the architecture or the data layer. Stewardship
        by a non-profit organization is the structural guarantee that community needs,
        not capital needs, set the product direction. The platform's open-source code,
        released under a license separate from this website, will permit anyone to
        fork and operate a competing social media service. That ensures community
        obligations are enforceable—not merely asserted in marketing copy. If the
        steward breaks the rules, users are not locked in: they can audit the code,
        depart, and sustain the mission from a fork. Feeds are not optimized for capital
        gains; they are engineered for human connection and well-being.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Success metric:</U> Under pure capitalism, the platform optimizes for
          ARPU and shareholder return. Under social capitalism, it optimizes for user
          well-being and community health.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Steward:</U> A for-profit board accountable to investors versus a non-profit
          organization accountable to its users and the public.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Corporate role:</U> Owner of the venue, the graph, and the defaults—versus
          guest, present by contribution and subject to the community's rules.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Privacy primitives:</U> Negotiable the moment they threaten revenue—versus
          non-negotiable structural requirements baked into the architecture.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The distinction is not rhetorical; it determines what can be built and
        what will survive. Under pure capitalism, every <a
          href="#part-i-the-new-dna-local-only-primitives"
          class="text-white underline decoration-zinc-600 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-white"
          >privacy primitive</a
        > documented in <a
          href="#part-i"
          class="text-white underline decoration-zinc-600 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-white"
          >Part I</a
        > is negotiable the moment it cannibalizes ad targeting. Under social capitalism,
        those primitives remain stable because the institution is not engineered to
        maximize shareholder value. Lawful compliance may require adaptation of any
        feature; that is true of every platform operating within the law. The difference
        is what else can trigger those reversals. Under pure capitalism, direct communication,
        local-only processing, and every other privacy primitive are features on loan—revocable
        when revenue demands it, not only when regulation does. Under social capitalism,
        the steward operates fully within regulation but holds a higher ethical boundary
        than a for-profit company: capital gain alone is never sufficient reason to
        abandon a primitive or sell out the users who depend on it.
      </p>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-5" number={5} title="Companies Last">
        <p>
          Social media should be for people first, and companies are simply
          guests in the conversation.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can look to the physical world for a healthier model of corporate
          participation. When a company wants a presence at a major human social
          event—such as a cultural festival, a city marathon, or a community
          gathering—that presence is not granted by default, nor do they get to
          own the venue. Instead, their presence is usually linked to a massive
          donation or financial contribution to support the event itself. They
          are acknowledged as sponsors and guests, permitted to participate only
          because they are helping to sustain the space for the people actually
          attending.
        </p>

        <p>
          The digital town square should operate on the exact same principle.
          Companies should not dictate the architecture, own the personal data,
          or dictate what users see in their feeds. If a brand wishes to
          participate in a human social network, their presence should be
          treated as a privilege, contingent on contributing to the open-source
          maintenance of the platform or the non-profit organization stewarding
          it. By relegating corporations to the status of contributing guests
          rather than architectural overlords, social media can finally
          prioritize the human connections it was built to foster.
        </p>

        <p>
          Under social capitalism, a brand's presence in that town square is a
          privilege earned by supporting the commons—through open-source
          maintenance, contributions to the non-profit stewarding the
          platform—not a right purchased with ad spend. That is the rebalancing:
          people first, companies last.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-vi" part="Part VI">
        Direct Communication and Corporate Accountability
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-vi-the-illusion-of-private-messaging"
        >The Illusion of Private Messaging</H3
      >
      <p>
        The tension between ad-driven capital gains and user privacy is starkest
        in how social media companies handle messaging. On 8 May 2026, Meta
        removed End-to-End Encryption (E2EE) from Instagram Direct Messages—an
        optional feature that was never the default and was buried deep in
        settings. Meta blamed "low adoption," even though most users were never
        meaningfully offered the choice. The company now directs users who want
        encrypted messaging to WhatsApp, consolidating private conversation
        inside a separate app while Instagram DMs revert to standard encryption,
        giving Meta technical access to message content. At the same time,
        Facebook started to roll out front door advertising hooks directly
        within its messaging interfaces, shifting interactions into commercial
        funnels.
      </p>

      <p>
        Child-safety advocates welcomed the Instagram change, arguing that
        platforms cannot detect grooming or abuse in conversations they cannot
        read. The timing also drew attention: the rollback came days before the
        United States Take It Down Act took full effect on 19 May 2026,
        requiring platforms to remove non-consensual intimate imagery—including
        AI-generated deepfakes—within 48 hours of notification. Compliance with
        laws like that is far easier when the platform holds the decryption
        keys.
      </p>

      <p>
        This exposes a fundamental truth about commercial social media: on these
        platforms, encryption was never a primitive. It was merely a toggleable
        UI feature—subject to commercial pressure, regulatory demands, and
        product convenience. Even when E2EE was nominally offered, these apps
        already had advertising hooks functioning as a front door to the ad
        engine. By removing E2EE entirely, the platforms simply drop the facade,
        ensuring they retain total technical access to your conversations to
        fuel their targeting networks.
      </p>

      <p>
        Platforms often argue that combating deepfakes, child grooming, fraud,
        harassment, non-consensual intimate imagery, and coordinated
        disinformation requires stripping away encryption and surveilling user
        communications. This is a false trade-off. These harms can only be truly
        addressed through accountability—cryptographically tying digital
        presence to a verified human identity, so there is always a real person
        behind an account who can be held responsible. Content moderation and
        keyword scanning at scale will always be reactive, incomplete, and
        easily gamed; structural accountability is what makes consequence
        possible in the first place.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-vi-honesty-transparency-and-accountability"
        >Honesty, Transparency, and Accountability</H3
      >
      <p>
        Direct communication—one-to-one and small-group messaging—is the most
        intimate layer of the social web. It is where people coordinate care,
        share vulnerabilities, and speak without an audience. When a corporation
        sits inside that channel, it inherits obligations that go far beyond a
        feed or a public timeline. From a corporate point of view, honesty means
        never implying privacy that the architecture does not provide. If a
        platform can read, index, or monetize a message, that must be stated
        plainly at the point of use—not buried in a policy update months after
        the product ships.
      </p>

      <p>
        Transparency is the operational counterpart to that honesty. A
        for-profit social company owes its shareholders growth; its incentives
        will always pull toward extracting signal from every interaction,
        including DMs. Corporate transparency therefore cannot be a marketing
        slogan or an annual transparency report that arrives after the damage is
        done. It must be structural: open-source clients, auditable server
        behavior, and defaults that make the limits of corporate access visible
        before a user sends the first message. When encryption is optional,
        buried, or revocable, the corporation is being transparent about one
        thing only—that user trust is negotiable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Accountability closes the loop. Corporations that promise private
        messaging and later remove it, insert advertising into conversation
        threads, or hand decrypted content to targeting systems should face real
        consequence—not a brief news cycle and a return to business as usual. In
        practice, commercial platforms treat these reversals as product
        decisions because no governing body holds them to their prior claims.
        That is why direct communication cannot remain a profit center inside
        the same corporate structure that optimizes feeds for outrage. The
        steward of human messaging must be accountable to users first: a
        non-profit organization, open governance, and cryptographic primitives
        that the company cannot silently switch off when regulation or revenue
        demands it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Brands that participate in direct communication should be held to the
        same standard. A corporate account is a guest in a human conversation,
        not an owner of the channel. Sponsored messages, automated outreach, and
        data collection from replies must be disclosed, limited, and subject to
        user control—never normalized as the default texture of private chat.
        Honesty about what the corporation sees, transparency about how it uses
        what it sees, and accountability when it breaks its own rules are not
        optional extras for a healthy social web. They are the minimum corporate
        behavior required before any company is allowed inside direct
        communication at all.
      </p>

      <Conclusion
        id="conclusion-6"
        number={6}
        title="Protecting Direct Communication"
      >
        <p>
          Direct messaging is not a side feature of social media—it is where
          trust is won or destroyed. Commercial platforms have shown repeatedly
          that when capital gains conflict with private conversation, the
          conversation loses.
        </p>

        <p>
          Protecting direct communication requires treating it as a human space
          first: end-to-end encryption as a non-negotiable primitive, no
          advertising hooks inside message threads, and corporate participation
          governed by honesty, transparency, and enforceable accountability. A
          company that cannot meet that bar should not be in the inbox at all.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-vii" part="Part VII">The Blueprint for a Private Web</H2>

      <H3 id="part-vii-synthesizing-the-vision">Synthesizing the Vision</H3>
      <p>
        The trajectory of the modern Internet is not an accident; it is the
        mathematical result of an open architecture colliding with the absolute
        demand for capital gains. To repair the digital town square, we cannot
        rely on the very corporations that profit from its degradation. We must
        address all the systemic failures concurrently:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must <U>improve the Web</U> by embedding local-only privacy primitives
          (like local-only feed building, secure cross-device synchronization, and
          encrypted backups) directly into its DNA.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must <U>add the "social" back into social media</U> by restoring user
          agency over the feed—reverse-chronological by default, with local-only algorithms
          available only as an explicit choice—rather than forcing outrage-optimized
          feeds designed for capital gains.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must <U>protect the vulnerable</U> by designing spaces that safely ease
          children into the digital world, rather than exploiting their developing
          minds.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must enforce a <U>people first, bots never</U> hierarchy by utilizing
          cryptographic identity protocols to eradicate synthetic propaganda, social
          media farms, and coordinated multi-account manipulation—and to enforce human
          accountability.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must put <U>companies last</U>, ensuring that corporate entities
          are treated as contributing guests rather than algorithmic dictators.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          We must <U>protect direct communication</U> by treating messaging as a human
          space—default end-to-end encryption, no ad hooks in conversation threads,
          and corporate honesty, transparency, and accountability before any brand
          is allowed inside the inbox.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Conclusion id="conclusion-final" final title="The Commitment to Build">
        <p>
          Identifying these problems is no longer sufficient. Writing about the
          necessary architecture is only the first step; the final step is to
          build it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Honest work remains on several fronts: age verification for minors is
          still in progress; a sustainable funding model for a non-profit
          platform must be proven in practice; and identity frameworks beyond
          the European Union will be integrated as they mature. This paper
          states the principles. The build will test them.
        </p>

        <p>
          To execute this vision, we are officially establishing a public and
          transparent non-profit Non-Governmental Organization (NGO). The sole
          mission of this organization will be to build, steward, and maintain a
          fundamentally new, open-source social media platform.
        </p>

        <p>
          This platform will be engineered from the ground up with the
          uncompromisable privacy primitives detailed in this paper. By
          operating as an NGO, we are permanently removing the structural need
          to maximize shareholder value. The code will be open, the algorithms
          will be transparent, and the computation of intent will remain
          strictly on your local devices.
        </p>

        <p>
          We are not building a platform to harvest your data, hook your
          attention, or optimize for outrage. We are building it entirely with
          the user's benefit in mind, deliberately rejecting the pursuit of
          "capital gains" that has corrupted the digital landscape. It is time
          to reclaim the digital town square and build a network that truly
          works for humanity.
        </p>
      </Conclusion>
    </section>
